  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thai omelette serves 1 and takes about 10 minutes. The key difference from a French omelette is the cooking method. This version is fried in a generous amount of hot oil so the edges puff and turn crispy and brown while the inside stays just set. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fish sauce",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "fish-sauce"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " goes directly into the beaten egg. Serve over jasmine rice with a drizzle of "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "oyster sauce",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "oyster-sauce"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Use more oil than you think you need. A Thai omelette is closer to a deep-fried egg than a pan-fried one. The hot oil makes the exterior lacework and gives the dish its texture."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
